    "result_text": "The full test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented a daily create-owner limit that rejects the request with **429 Too Many Requests** once 100 or more owners have already been created today (counted by `registrationDate`).\n\nFollowing the existing \"check\" pattern (`CheckOwnerCityCapacity`), I added:\n\n1. **`CheckOwnerDailyLimit.java`** (`rest/function/owner/`) \u2014 counts owners whose `registrationDate` equals the server's current date via `OwnerRepository.findAll()`, and throws a checked `OwnerDailyLimitException` when the count reaches the limit of 100.\n\n2. **`OwnerDailyLimitException.java`** (`rest/escalation/`) \u2014 a checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it appears in the `throws` clause and is matched by escalation routing.\n\n3. **`OwnerDailyLimitExceptionHandler.java`** (`rest/escalation/`) \u2014 responds `429` with body `{\"errors\":[\"registrationDate\"]}`.\n\n4. **Escalation YAML** \u2014 `officefloor/escalation/\u2026OwnerDailyLimitException.yml` wiring the exception to its handler.\n\n5. **Pipeline wiring** \u2014 inserted a `checkDailyLimit` step into `owners.POST.yml` right after `validate` (and under `govern: [ transaction ]` like its siblings), so the limit is enforced before any owner is built or saved.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` passes (creates 100 owners, then the 101st returns 429), and the full suite builds green.",
